  "raw_meta_yaml": "schema_version: 1\n\ncontext:\n  version: \"0.1.2\"\n\npackage:\n  name: onionize\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  url: https://github.com/AndSemenoff/onionize/archive/refs/tags/v${{ version }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: 8129daeec5f1d5340b0a73c5da1277d0e45d1baf5e35237a48f7b0bb295a24a3\n\nbuild:\n  number: 0\n  script:\n    env:\n      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_STRIP: symbols\n      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_LTO: fat\n      # rusqlite requests `features = [\"bundled\"]`, which compiles a vendored\n      # SQLite. This overrides it so libsqlite3-sys links conda-forge's\n      # libsqlite instead and SQLite fixes arrive through the package manager.\n      LIBSQLITE3_SYS_USE_PKG_CONFIG: \"1\"\n    content:\n      - if: unix\n        then:\n          - cargo auditable install --locked --no-track --bin onionize --root ${{ PREFIX }} --path .\n        else:\n          # libsqlite3-sys only consults PKG_CONFIG_PATH on MSVC when it is\n          # pointed at a lib directory explicitly.\n          - set \"SQLITE3_LIB_DIR=%LIBRARY_LIB%\"\n          - set \"SQLITE3_INCLUDE_DIR=%LIBRARY_INC%\"\n          - cargo auditable install --locked --no-track --bin onionize --root %LIBRARY_PREFIX% --path .\n      - cargo-bundle-licenses --format yaml --output ./THIRDPARTY.yml\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - ${{ compiler('c') }}\n    - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n    - ${{ compiler('rust') }}\n    - cargo-bundle-licenses\n    - cargo-auditable\n    - pkg-config\n  host:\n    # native-tls uses OpenSSL on Linux, Security.framework on macOS and\n    # SChannel on Windows.\n    - if: linux\n      then: openssl\n    - libsqlite\n    # arti pulls in liblzma-sys for directory compression. It only looks for a\n    # system liblzma through pkg-config; on MSVC it skips the probe and always\n    # compiles its vendored xz, so the headers are of no use there.\n    - if: not win\n      then: liblzma-devel\n\ntests:\n  - script:\n      - onionize --help\n      - onionize --version\n  - package_contents:\n      bin:\n        - onionize\n      strict: true\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://github.com/AndSemenoff/onionize\n  repository: https://github.com/AndSemenoff/onionize\n  documentation: https://docs.rs/onionize\n  license: MIT OR Apache-2.0\n  license_file:\n    - LICENSE-APACHE\n    - LICENSE-MIT\n    - THIRDPARTY.yml\n  summary: A CLI tool for creating ephemeral Tor Onion Services to proxy local ports\n  description: |\n    onionize takes a local port and instantly creates a temporary .onion\n    address for it. No registration, no central servers, no NAT configuration\n    required. Think of it as a decentralized, private alternative to ngrok,\n    powered by Arti, the Tor implementation in Rust.\n\n    Because it embeds the Arti client, no torrc editing or separate tor daemon\n    is needed. Services can be restricted to authorized clients using x25519\n    keys, and a QR code can be generated to open the link in Tor Browser for\n    mobile.\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - pavelzw\n",
